diagnosis_text:
The network is operating correctly. A comprehensive inspection found no fault anywhere in the reported traffic path.

The claimed symptom — Office LAN (192.168.3.0/24) unable to reach the Server Room (1.0.0.0/8) and Internet Lab (128.168.0.0/16) — was tested exhaustively and does not correspond to any real failure. All five Office hosts (office_pc, enquiry, exam_cell, tpo, plus the Principle LAN) can reach pc1 (1.0.0.5) and pc2/pc3 (128.168.0.2) with 0% packet loss. Traceroute from office_pc confirms the expected three-hop path: Office LAN → router1 (192.168.3.1) → router2 (20.20.0.2) → destination.

Every component in the forwarding chain was verified: router1 and router2 both have correct RIP-learned routes (router1 holds R>* 1.0.0.0/8 via 20.20.0.2 and R>* 128.168.0.0/16 via 20.20.0.2; router2 holds R>* 192.168.3.0/24 via 20.20.0.1); all interfaces are UP/UP with correct IP assignments; IP forwarding is enabled on both routers (ip_forward=1); no iptables rules exist on any device; and host default gateways are correctly pointed at router1.eth0 (192.168.3.1) and router2.eth0/eth1. RIP version 2 is active and fully converged on all three routers with normal 30-second update timers.

The helpdesk ticket describes a fault that does not exist in the current state of the lab. No corrective action is required.
